The documentation explains how to use the Jakarta EE standard annotation @Resource (jakarta.annotation.Resource) for dependency injection by bean name.
While @Autowired is fundamentally by-type first (looking for what class matches), @Resource is fundamentally by-name first (looking for a unique bean ID match).

1. Explicit Selection By-Name

The most robust way to use @Resource is to supply a name attribute. Spring goes into its context container and directly retrieves the bean matching that exact ID.
java
public class SimpleMovieLister {

    @Resource(name="myMovieFinder") // <-- Target the exact bean named "myMovieFinder"
    private MovieFinder movieFinder;
}
Use code with caution.

2. Implicit Selection (The Default Fallback)

If you omit the name attribute, Spring will automatically deduce the target bean name from the name of the Java variable or the setter method name you applied it to.
java
public class SimpleMovieLister {

    @Resource 
    private MovieFinder movieFinder; // <-- Spring will implicitly look for a bean named "movieFinder"
}
Use code with caution.

3. The Resolution Protocol: What if the name doesn't exist?

If you did not provide an explicit name, and Spring cannot find a bean matching the variable's name, it activates a backup resolution protocol:
1. Search by Variable Name: First, it tries to match the exact field name against your bean IDs.
2. Fallback to Primary Type: If no name matches, it shifts behaviors to act like @Autowired by searching for a type match, favoring any bean marked @Primary.
3. Internal Context Resolution: It seamlessly maps built-in structural framework beans if the type matches interfaces like ApplicationContext or BeanFactory.
java
public class MovieRecommender {
    @Resource
    private CustomerPreferenceDao customerPreferenceDao; // Checks for name "customerPreferenceDao" first, then defaults to type match.

    @Resource
    private ApplicationContext context; // Automatically resolves the framework context bean based on its type!
}
Use code with caution.

4. Structural Restrictions

A key detail mentioned in earlier parts of the documentation context is that @Resource is structurally more limited than @Autowired:
• Supported targets: @Resource can only be placed directly on fields or single-argument setter methods.
• Unsupported targets: You cannot use @Resource on class constructors or on methods that require multiple parameters. If you need constructor injection, you must stick with @Autowired.

