The documentation explains how to use the Jakarta EE standard annotations @PostConstruct and @PreDestroy to hook into a Spring bean's lifecycle.
These annotations allow you to trigger custom code automatically right after a bean is fully initialized, or right before it is destroyed by the application container.

1. @PostConstruct (The Setup Hook)

When Spring creates a bean, it runs the constructor and then injects all requested dependencies (via @Autowired, @Value, etc.).
If you try to run initialization logic inside the class constructor that relies on those dependencies, you will get a NullPointerException because Spring hasn't injected them yet. @PostConstruct solves this by executing immediately after dependency injection is complete.

Common Use Cases:

• Pre-loading or seeding data into a cache.
• Establishing connection pools or initializing third-party libraries.
• Validating that crucial properties were correctly loaded via @Value. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/annotation-config/postconstruct-and-predestroy-annotations.html#:~:text=%2C%20the%20cache%20is%20pre%2Dpopulated,initialization%20and%20cleared%20upon%20destruction)
java
@Component
public class DatabaseManager {

    @Autowired
    private DataSource dataSource; // Spring injects this first

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        // Runs automatically AFTER dataSource is completely ready
        System.out.println("Connecting to database and verifying schema...");
    }
}
Use code with caution.

2. @PreDestroy (The Cleanup Hook)

When your application is closing down, Spring destroys all active beans. @PreDestroy lets you execute logic immediately before the bean is removed from memory.

Common Use Cases:

• Gracefully closing open database connections or file streams.
• Saving unsaved session states or flushing an in-memory queue to disk.
• Safely shutting down background thread pools.
java
@Component
public class DatabaseManager {

    @PreDestroy
    public void cleanup() {
        // Runs automatically right before the application shuts down
        System.out.println("Closing all active database connections safely...");
    }
}
