The documentation explains Classpath Scanning, which is Spring’s mechanism for automatically discovering your classes and registering them as beans in the application container, eliminating the need to declare them one by one in XML or configuration files.

1. Stereotype Annotations (The Markers)

To make a Java class eligible for automatic discovery, you label it with a stereotype annotation. Spring provides four main tiers:
• @Component: The generic, root stereotype annotation for any Spring-managed class.
• @Service: A specialization of @Component meant for business logic.
• @Repository: Built for data persistence (DAOs). It also automatically translates database-specific errors into Spring's data exception hierarchy.
• @Controller: Reserved for handling presentation and web requests (like Spring MVC).

2. Turning Scanning On: @ComponentScan

Simply adding annotations to your classes isn't enough; you must explicitly tell Spring where to look. You activate this by placing @ComponentScan on a configuration class:
java
@Configuration
@ComponentScan(basePackages = "com.example.myapp") // Scopes the search
public class AppConfig { }
Use code with caution.
Spring will scan the specified package and all of its sub-packages. It looks for the stereotype annotations, generates a BeanDefinition for each class it finds, and registers them into the container automatically.

3. Advanced Filtering Rules

By default, @ComponentScan only looks for the main stereotype annotations. However, you can use includeFilters and excludeFilters to customize this behavior completely:
java
@Configuration
@ComponentScan(
    basePackages = "org.example",
    includeFilters = @Filter(type = FilterType.REGEX, pattern = ".*Stub.*Repository"), // Include matching regex
    excludeFilters = @Filter(Repository.class) // Block standard repositories
)
public class AppConfig { }
Use code with caution.
Spring offers 5 filter types to fine-tune your scanning patterns:
1. annotation: (Default) Filters based on the presence of a specific class-level annotation.
2. assignable: Filters based on whether a class extends a specific base class or implements a specific interface.
3. aspectj: Matches targets using complex AspectJ type expressions.
4. regex: Matches against fully qualified class names using standard Regular Expressions.
5. custom: Allows you to plug in a programmatic filter by implementing Spring's TypeFilter interface.

4. Automatic Bean Naming

When Spring registers an autodetected component, it has to assign it a bean name:
• Custom Names: If you supply a string argument to the stereotype—like @Service("myLister")—Spring uses that name.
• Default Names: If unnamed, Spring defaults to the class name but lowercases the first letter (e.g., MovieFinderImpl becomes movieFinderImpl).
• Handling Name Collisions: If two different packages contain classes with identical names (e.g., com.packageA.Service and com.packageB.Service), you can override the default naming strategy by passing FullyQualifiedAnnotationBeanNameGenerator.class into your @ComponentScan parameters to make bean IDs unique.

5. Defining @Bean Inside a @Component

A fascinating design detail highlighted in the text is that plain @Component classes can also house @Bean factory methods. However, they behave completely differently than @Bean methods inside a @Configuration class:
• In a @Configuration class (Full Mode): Spring uses CGLIB proxying to intercept cross-method calls. If method A calls method B, Spring intercepts it to return the exact same scoped bean instance instead of executing the Java method again.
• In a @Component class (Lite Mode): No CGLIB proxying occurs. If you programmatically call another @Bean method within the same @Component, it executes with standard Java semantics and will create a completely new, independent instance right then and there.
