JSR-330 is the official Java standard specification for Dependency Injection. Starting with Spring 3.0, Spring built native support for these standard annotations. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/5.3.13/reference/html/core.html), [2] (https://www.spring-certification.com/post/understanding-dependency-injection-annotations-in-spring), [3] (https://jcp.org/en/jsr/detail?id=330), [4] (https://mkyong.com/spring3/spring-3-and-jsr-330-inject-and-named-example/)
They live inside the jakarta.inject package (historically javax.inject in older versions of Java). They allow you to write framework-agnostic code. If you use JSR-330 annotations, your classes can theoretically be migrated from Spring to other DI containers (like Google Guice or Quarkus) without changing your Java source imports. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/standard-annotations.html), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3flORdmpxhk), [3] (https://mkyong.com/spring3/spring-3-and-jsr-330-inject-and-named-example/), [4] (https://www.spring-certification.com/post/understanding-dependency-injection-annotations-in-spring)

1. @Inject (The Standard Alternative to @Autowired)

The @Inject annotation performs type-based dependency injection exactly like Spring's @Autowired. It can be placed on fields, constructors, or methods. [1] (https://juliuskrah.com/blog/2016/12/10/introduction-to-spring-and-dependency-injection-jsr-330/), [2] (https://www.spring-certification.com/post/understanding-dependency-injection-annotations-in-spring)
java
import jakarta.inject.Inject;

public class MovieRecommender {

    private final MovieCatalog movieCatalog;

    @Inject // <-- Replaces Spring's @Autowired
    public MovieRecommender(MovieCatalog movieCatalog) {
        this.movieCatalog = movieCatalog;
    }
}
Use code with caution.

Key Differences from @Autowired:

• No required attribute: @Autowired(required = false) allows optional dependencies. @Inject lacks a built-in required parameter.
• Handling optional values: If you need a dependency to be optional while using @Inject, you must use Java's native Optional<T> wrapper class instead: [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/standard-annotations.html)
java
@Inject
private Optional<MovieCatalog> movieCatalog;
Use code with caution.

2. @Named (The Standard Alternative to @Component / @Qualifier)

The @Named annotation serves a dual purpose in JSR-330 depending on where it is placed: [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/standard-annotations.html)

Usage A: Component Declaration (Like @Component)

When placed at the class level, it registers the class as a discovered component during classpath scanning. [1] (https://mkyong.com/spring3/spring-3-and-jsr-330-inject-and-named-example/), [2] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/standard-annotations.html)
java
import jakarta.inject.Named;

@Named("mainCatalog") // <-- Replaces Spring's @Component("mainCatalog")
public class SimpleMovieCatalog implements MovieCatalog { ... }
Use code with caution.
• The Limitation: Unlike Spring’s @Component, @Named is not composable. You cannot use @Named as a meta-annotation to build custom stereotype layers (like how Spring uses @Component to create @Service or @Repository). [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/standard-annotations.html)

Usage B: Injection Targeting (Like @Qualifier)

When placed at an injection point, it acts as a literal string qualifier to narrow down candidates. [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/standard-annotations.html)
java
@Inject
@Named("mainCatalog") // <-- Replaces Spring's @Qualifier("mainCatalog")
private MovieCatalog movieCatalog;
Use code with caution.

Comparison Summary & Restrictions

When using the JSR-330 standard inside a Spring context, certain features require careful mapping due to spec limitations: [1] (https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/standard-annotations.html)

Spring Annotation	JSR-330 Equivalent	Notes & Restrictions
@Autowired	@Inject	Lacks a required flag. Use Optional<T>.
@Component	@Named	Works with component scanning, but isn't structurally composable.
@Qualifier("id")	@Named("id")	Standard string matching. JSR-330's base @Qualifier is strictly a meta-annotation for custom tags.
@Scope("singleton")	@Singleton	The native JSR-330 specification defaults to prototype scope. However, Spring overrides this to enforce its default singleton behavior for consistency.
@Value	None	No standard equivalent exists. You must use Spring's @Value for properties.
@Lazy	None	No standard equivalent exists. You must use Spring's @Lazy.

